==German==
{{wikipedia|lang=de}}

===Etymology===
15th century ([[East Prussia]]n), also {{m|de||vluder}}, from {{der|de|gml|vlundere}}, from {{der|de|osx|*flunthria}}, eventually from {{der|de|gem-pro|*flunþrijǭ}}. 

The [[High German]] word must be from [[Low German]] given the marine context. However, Low German attestations are equally late and there are some indications that the word was not originally widely known. It may have been regionally restricted, or otherwise a borrowing from {{der|de|non|flyðra}} and/or {{der|de|enm|flounder}}.
<!--Kluge mentions a 16th-century source that calls it an English word. However, it doesn't say whether this source is northern German. If not, it wouldn't mean much.-->

